---
title: "Visible Rows"
---

When a server streams live updates to the grid, it is wasteful to send updates for rows the user cannot see. `subscribeToVisibleRows` tells your application which rows to subscribe to, and which to unsubscribe from, as rows enter and leave the view.

{% apiDocumentation source="grid-api/api.json" section="displayedRows" names=["subscribeToVisibleRows"] /%}

Subscribing to visible rows requires the `VisibleRowsModule` and [Row IDs](./row-ids/), and is supported by the [Client-Side Row Model](./row-models/#client-side) and the [Server-Side Row Model](./server-side-model/) {% enterpriseIcon /%}.

## Handlers

The method takes a pair of handlers and returns a function that stops the subscription.

```{% frameworkTransform=true %}
const stopVisibleRowsSubscription = api.subscribeToVisibleRows({
    onSubscribe: (rows, params) => {
        stream.subscribe(rows.map((row) => row.id));
    },
    onUnsubscribe: (rows, params) => {
        stream.unsubscribe(rows.map((row) => row.id));
    },
});

// later
stopVisibleRowsSubscription();
```

- `onSubscribe` receives `VisibleRow` objects, which include the row `data` and whether the row is a `group`.
- `onUnsubscribe` receives `VisibleRowRef` objects, which hold only the `id`, `parentKeys`, `level` and, for rows that have a key, the `route`. These are captured when the row is subscribed, as the row node may no longer exist when it is unsubscribed.
- Every subscribed row is unsubscribed exactly once, including when the subscription is stopped or the grid is destroyed. When the grid is destroyed, the rows are unsubscribed while the API is still usable.
- When rows change, `onUnsubscribe` is called before `onSubscribe`.
- A row moved to another parent, for example by a transaction that changes its group or data path, is unsubscribed with its old `parentKeys` and `route`, then subscribed with the new ones.
- New row data keeps the rows whose IDs are still present, so only the dropped rows are unsubscribed.

## Which Rows Are Included

Only loaded rows in view are passed to the handlers. The following rows are never passed:

- [Loading placeholder rows](./server-side-model-loading-rows/) and [rows that failed to load](./server-side-model-retry/).
- [Pinned Rows](./row-pinning/).
- [Detail Rows](./master-detail/) and [Full Width Rows](./full-width-rows/).
- [Group total (footer) rows and the grand total row](./aggregation-total-rows/).

## Options

{% interfaceDocumentation interfaceName="VisibleRowsOptions" config={ "description": "" } /%}

- Set `includeBuffer` to `true` to also subscribe to rows rendered in the row buffer, so updates are ready before the rows scroll into view.
- Set `includeGroups` to `false` if the stream only has updates for leaf rows.
- Set `throttleMs` to limit how often the handlers are called while the user scrolls, so updates keep arriving during a scroll at a rate the stream can handle.
- Set `debounceMs` to wait until scrolling stops before calling the handlers, so rows that are scrolled past quickly, for example by dragging the scrollbar, are never subscribed. This suits streams where each subscription is costly.
- Set both to wait for scrolling to stop, but call the handlers at least every `throttleMs` during a long, slow scroll.

```{% frameworkTransform=true %}
api.subscribeToVisibleRows(handlers, {
    includeBuffer: true,
    debounceMs: 150,
    throttleMs: 1000,
    includeGroups: false,
});
```

{% note %}
Sticky group rows are unsubscribed once their row scrolls above the visible range, even while they stay stuck to the top of the grid. With the Server-Side Row Model, a refresh that does not purge can unsubscribe rows and then subscribe them again.
{% /note %}

## Examples

### Row Grouping {% enterpriseIcon /%}

The example below [groups](./grouping/) stocks by sector. The handlers send the subscribed row IDs over a simulated websocket, which streams price updates for the subscribed stocks only. The updates are applied with [Async Transactions](./data-update-high-frequency/).

- The panel shows the number of subscribed rows and the number of updates the server has sent. Each batch is logged to the console with its row count.
- Scrolling, sorting and filtering produce subscribe and unsubscribe batches.
- Changing the grouping in the [Columns Tool Panel](./tool-panel-columns/) moves rows to new parents, so those rows are unsubscribed and subscribed again.
- **Include buffer** and **Include group rows** set the `includeBuffer` and `includeGroups` options. Changing either stops the subscription and subscribes again.
- **New Row Data** replaces every stock with a new ID, so the old stocks are unsubscribed and the new stocks subscribed.
- **Stop** stops the subscription and so the stream, unsubscribing all remaining rows. **Start** subscribes again, passing the rows in view.

{% gridExampleRunner title="Visible Rows with Row Grouping" name="client-side-visible-rows" exampleHeight=615 /%}

### Server-Side Tree Data {% enterpriseIcon /%}

The example below shows a portfolio of regions, sectors and stocks as [Tree Data](./server-side-model-tree-data/). Rows are loaded over a simulated websocket, and the same socket streams price updates for the subscribed stocks only. The updates are applied with [Asynchronous Transactions](./server-side-model-updating-transactions/#asynchronous-updates), using each row's `parentKeys` as the route.

- Scrolling, expanding groups and loading new blocks produce subscribe and unsubscribe batches, logged to the console with their row count.
- **Purge** reloads all rows. The old rows are unsubscribed, and the new rows subscribed as they load.
- **Stop** stops the subscription and so the stream, unsubscribing all remaining rows. **Start** subscribes again, passing the rows in view.

{% gridExampleRunner title="Visible Rows with Server-Side Tree Data" name="server-side-visible-rows" exampleHeight=615 /%}
